export const LESSONS = [
  // Module 1 — Purchasing Team Foundation
  lesson('pur-m1-l1', 'pur-m1', 1, 'What the Purchasing Team Is Responsible For',
    'The sources describe the Purchasing Team as the group that creates and maintains suppliers, creates and updates Purchase Orders, manages product and compliance information, assigns orders to containers, and keeps the Purchasing Dashboard accurate throughout. A separate Ordering Policy source frames the same team\'s job as ensuring every PO is created based on sales performance, profitability, warehouse capacity, supplier lead times, and operational needs — while minimising excess inventory and improving stock availability. Together these describe a team that owns the full life of a Purchase Order, not just the moment it is created. A mistake to avoid: treating PO creation as a single, isolated task — the sources describe it as one step inside a longer chain that includes supplier setup, compliance documentation, container assignment, and ongoing dashboard accuracy.',
    5, 'Purchase Order Update Procedure — Purchasing Dashboard SOP, Purpose section; Purchase Order Ordering Policy SOP, Purpose section'),
  lesson('pur-m1-l2', 'pur-m1', 2, 'Purchasing and "Inventory Purchasing": What Is Actually Established',
    'The source material does not define an organisational boundary between "Purchasing" and "Inventory Purchasing" as two separate teams or roles — no document uses that second phrase in its written text to describe a team, a job title, or a reporting line. What the sources do establish is that a set of decision-support and scoring tools (rules, specifications, and workbooks used to help judge whether a product should be reordered) is grouped together and referred to by that label. This programme treats "Inventory Purchasing" as the name given to that decision-support toolset, not as a confirmed second team, and does not invent a boundary the sources do not state. A mistake to avoid: assuming a specific person or team owns "Inventory Purchasing" work — the sources simply do not say.',
    4, 'Programme-wide finding — no source under Purchasing_Team/ defines an organisational boundary between "Purchasing" and "Inventory Purchasing"; see Programme Sources'),
  lesson('pur-m1-l3', 'pur-m1', 3, 'Records and Evidence the Team Relies On',
    'Several source documents describe specific records that must exist and be kept current: a record of supplier login details, a Purchase Order reference number for every order, CBM (volume) verification records confirmed before a product is ordered, an Incident Log for supplier-related issues, and a Purchase Order Remarks section for anything a supplier or agent must remember, follow, or verify. The sources are explicit on why this matters: "verbal instructions alone are not sufficient — all important instructions must be documented for traceability and accountability." This is the thread that runs through every module in this programme — a decision or instruction that exists only as a conversation is treated as if it never happened. A mistake to avoid: assuming a verbal confirmation from a supplier or agent is enough on its own — it must be written down somewhere the whole team can see.',
    4, 'Purchase Order Update Procedure — Purchasing Dashboard SOP, Purchase Order Remarks Requirements section'),
  lesson('pur-m1-l4', 'pur-m1', 4, 'Confidentiality and Record Accuracy',
    'Because Purchasing records touch real suppliers, real costs, and real order data, this programme never uses real supplier names, real PO numbers, real prices, or any other live business data in its lessons, examples, or exercises — every example throughout this programme is fictional. The sources reinforce the same discipline internally: when a compliance document like an Instruction Manual or GSPR Label is updated, the rule is that "old versions must not be used after approval of new versions," and every affected party must be informed and confirm receipt of the new version. The underlying principle is the same one from the previous lesson — accuracy and traceability matter more than convenience. A mistake to avoid: continuing to reference or use an older version of a document or a piece of data after a newer, approved version exists.',
    4, 'Purchase Order Update Procedure — Purchasing Dashboard SOP, Document Change Management section'),

  // Module 2 — End-to-End Purchasing Workflow
  lesson('pur-m2-l1', 'pur-m2', 1, 'From Purchasing Need to Purchase Order',
    'The sources describe purchasing need in terms of sales performance: products are classified by how much of total sales they represent, and that classification is what tells the team whether, and how urgently, to reorder. Before a Purchase Order is ever created, the sources require reviewing sales data, checking supplier lead-time and minimum-order-quantity requirements, and confirming that product information is current in the system. Only once these checks are complete does the workflow move to actually building the PO. A mistake to avoid: jumping straight to entering a Purchase Order before the underlying sales and readiness checks are done — the sources present these checks as a required first step, not an optional one.',
    5, 'Purchase Order Ordering Policy SOP, Purchase Order Creation Flow section'),
  lesson('pur-m2-l2', 'pur-m2', 2, 'Approval Before Anything Moves',
    'Every source in this programme that describes a Purchase Order reaching a decision point uses the same shape of rule: work can be completed, but it cannot proceed to the next stage until a defined approval step has happened. A completed Purchase Order must be reviewed by the Warehouse Team before it can be entered into the system at all — the sources are explicit that "purchase orders can only be added to the system after warehouse approval." This same pattern — build, check, then wait for approval — reappears at every later stage of this programme, including order confirmation and shipping. A mistake to avoid: treating warehouse review as a formality that can be skipped when a team member is confident the order is correct — the source states this approval is required, not optional.',
    5, 'Purchase Order Ordering Policy SOP, Warehouse & Operational Requirements section'),
  lesson('pur-m2-l3', 'pur-m2', 3, 'Supplier Follow-Up Through to Shipping',
    'Once an order is placed, the sources describe a specific follow-up chain rather than leaving it to check in "when convenient": the supplier\'s acknowledgement of the order is verified, an Expected Completion Date is tracked, and any order that runs past that date is treated as a distinct, named condition requiring follow-up. When a container is finally loaded and confirmed, the source instructs updating the order\'s status to a specific value in both the Purchasing Dashboard and the Inventory Dashboard. This chain of checkpoints is what turns "the order was placed" into "the order is confirmed on its way," with a documented status at every step. A mistake to avoid: assuming a placed order needs no further attention until it is due to arrive — the sources describe active, staged monitoring in between.',
    4, 'Order Follow-Up, Production Monitoring & Container Loading Policy SOP, Order Acknowledgement Verification and Shipping Status Update sections'),
  lesson('pur-m2-l4', 'pur-m2', 4, 'Stock Receipt Through to Closure',
    'The final stage of the shared workflow is receiving the container and confirming what was ordered is what arrived: verifying the container and seal numbers, checking cartons against the Packing List, and reporting any shortage or damage immediately rather than after the fact. The receiving source is explicit that this process is only considered complete once every item on its own checklist has been confirmed — closure is a defined end-state, not simply "the container has arrived." Some details of this shared sequence differ across sources in ways that are not resolved here (for example, exact container-capacity figures) — those specific points are deliberately left out of this programme and are listed on the Programme Sources page rather than taught as settled fact. A mistake to avoid: treating "the container arrived" as the same thing as "the receipt process is complete" — the sources treat them as two separate milestones.',
    4, 'Container Arrival, Stock Receipt & Inventory Update Policy SOP, Container Receipt Procedure and Final Receiving Checklist sections'),

  // Module 3 — Purchase Requirement and Inventory Inputs
  lesson('pur-m3-l1', 'pur-m3', 1, 'Sales-Based Classification: Core, Middle, and End-of-Line',
    'The Ordering Policy source sorts products into three bands by their share of total sales, and each band gets a different reordering rule. Products making up roughly the top 80% of total sales are classified as Core products and should maintain about 12 months of stock coverage. Products in the lowest 5% of sales are reviewed for End-of-Line status, and ordering quantities for them should be minimised or the product discontinued where appropriate. Products in between these two bands sit in a middle tier that is reviewed on its own terms rather than being treated as automatically Core or automatically End-of-Line. A mistake to avoid: treating every product the same way regardless of its sales share — the whole point of this classification is that a top-selling product and a bottom-tier product are managed differently.',
    5, 'Purchase Order Ordering Policy SOP, Sales-Based Ordering section (1.1–1.2)'),
  lesson('pur-m3-l2', 'pur-m3', 2, 'Requested Quantity: Carton Multiples and Minimum Order Quantity',
    'Two separate rules govern how much of a product can actually be ordered once a need is identified. First, the order quantity must always be entered as a whole multiple of the product\'s Carton Pack Quantity (CTN PCS) — for a product with a Carton Pack Quantity of 12, valid order quantities include 12, 24, 36, 48, and 60, while 15, 25, or 37 would not be valid, because they do not divide evenly by the carton size. Second, every supplier\'s Minimum Order Quantity (MOQ) must be reviewed before creating a Purchase Order; if the amount actually needed is below the supplier\'s MOQ, the sources require reviewing alternative products or consolidating orders, and obtaining management approval before proceeding with a lower quantity than the MOQ. A mistake to avoid: rounding an order quantity to a number that looks "close enough" instead of an exact carton multiple.',
    5, 'Purchase Order Ordering Policy SOP, Order Quantity Rules section (11.1–11.2)'),
  lesson('pur-m3-l3', 'pur-m3', 3, 'Supplier and Product Readiness Inputs',
    'Before ordering, the sources require knowing how long a supplier typically takes to deliver, since this affects how far in advance stock must be planned. The Ordering Policy describes two lead-time bands: a standard lead time that applies to most suppliers, and a longer lead time assigned to specific suppliers whose production takes more time — this programme does not name which suppliers fall into which band, since supplier identity is excluded from learner content, but the concept of planning further ahead for a longer-lead-time supplier is a real, source-backed input to the purchasing decision. Historical return rates are also reviewed before placing an order, and order quantities are adjusted based on a product or supplier\'s quality and return performance. A mistake to avoid: using the same planning lead time for every supplier regardless of their actual track record.',
    4, 'Purchase Order Ordering Policy SOP, Supplier Planning Rules and Return Rate Review sections'),
  lesson('pur-m3-l4', 'pur-m3', 4, 'Data-Quality Checks Before Ordering',
    'The sources are explicit that a purchasing decision must not be made on data that has not been confirmed current. Before any product is added to a Purchase Order, the volume (CBM) information for that product must be confirmed as updated in the system, and a product without verified CBM details must not be ordered — the source warns that incorrect volume data can cause a meaningful gap between what was planned and what actually happens once the order is loaded. The same discipline applies to product information generally: specifications, images, and identifiers must be complete and correct, and any product with missing or unclear information must not be approved. A mistake to avoid: proceeding with an order because a product "usually" has correct data on file — the source requires the data to be confirmed current for that specific order, every time.',
    4, 'Purchase Order Ordering Policy SOP, Container & CBM Planning section (2.3); Purchase Order Update Procedure — Purchasing Dashboard SOP, Product Information & Order File Verification section'),

  // Module 4 — Purchase Order Creation and Ordering Policy
  lesson('pur-m4-l1', 'pur-m4', 1, 'Required PO Fields and Supplier and Product Details',
    'Before a Purchase Order is created, the source requires a defined set of checks to already be confirmed: supplier information, product details, quantities, pricing, the total order value, delivery dates, and container allocation. After the order is created, the checks continue — confirming every intended product is actually included, saving the PO reference number, and verifying it has been assigned to the correct container. This two-sided check (before creation and after creation) is what the source means by treating PO creation as a controlled process rather than a single data-entry step. A mistake to avoid: checking these fields only once, before creation — the source explicitly repeats several of the same checks immediately after the order is built, to catch anything that changed or was missed.',
    5, 'Purchase Order Update Procedure — Purchasing Dashboard SOP, Purchase Order Creation section'),
  lesson('pur-m4-l2', 'pur-m4', 2, 'The Purchase Order ID Naming Convention',
    'Every Purchase Order ID follows a fixed structure: the supplier\'s code, a two-digit month (01 to 12), an order-sequence number, a four-digit year, and — only for certain markets — a marketplace code. The first order placed with a supplier in a given month carries no sequence number; the second order that month is marked 2, the third is marked 3, and so on. A marketplace code is only required for specific markets named in the source; for the United Kingdom, no marketplace code is added at all. Before using any PO ID, the source requires verifying that the supplier code, month, sequence number, year, and marketplace code (where required) are all correct, and that the ID has not already been used. A mistake to avoid: adding a sequence number to the very first order of the month for a supplier — that first order is left unnumbered under this convention.',
    5, 'Purchase Order Update Procedure — Purchasing Dashboard SOP, PO ID Naming Convention section'),
  lesson('pur-m4-l3', 'pur-m4', 3, 'Compliance Documentation Before Approval',
    'Certain product types require specific compliance documents to be uploaded to the Purchasing Dashboard before a Purchase Order can be approved: an Instruction Manual and a compliance safety label, both written in clear, correct language and matching the product\'s actual specifications and the requirements of the market it is being sold into. The source includes one very specific prohibition worth remembering exactly as written: a particular safety certification symbol must never appear on either document "unless officially approved and required" — using it without that approval is treated as a compliance error, not a stylistic choice. A mistake to avoid: uploading a compliance document that was correct for a previous, similar product without re-checking that it still matches this specific product\'s specifications.',
    5, 'Purchase Order Update Procedure — Purchasing Dashboard SOP, Prepack Product Compliance Requirements and GSPR Label Requirements sections'),
  lesson('pur-m4-l4', 'pur-m4', 4, 'Errors to Avoid Before Submission',
    'The source calls out several specific mistakes that must be caught before a Purchase Order is approved: products with missing or unclear images must not be approved; accidental duplicate SKU entries must be identified and removed (while intentional duplicate lines, correctly quantified, are fine); and where a product costs noticeably more than a standard version of the same item, its expected value must be checked against separately-held pricing data before ordering — if that check does not support the order, it must not proceed without management approval. Every one of these is framed the same way in the source: a specific, named condition that stops the order until it is resolved, not a general reminder to "be careful." A mistake to avoid: treating an unresolved image, duplicate-SKU, or value-check issue as something that can be fixed after approval — the source requires resolving each one first.',
    5, 'Purchase Order Update Procedure — Purchasing Dashboard SOP, Product Information & Order File Verification and Duplicate Order Verification sections; Purchasing Rule Book, Electro Colour Products section'),

  // Module 5 — Purchase Order Updates and Change Control
  lesson('pur-m5-l1', 'pur-m5', 1, 'When a Purchase Order Must Be Updated',
    'The source describes a defined set of triggers for updating a Purchase Order after it has already been created: changes to Instruction Manuals, safety labels, product specifications, product warnings, packaging information, or other compliance information. Each of these triggers the same required sequence — update the latest version in the Dashboard, inform the Agent, inform the Supplier, and confirm that both have received and understood the change. This is deliberately more than just editing a field: the source treats a PO update as an event that other people need to know about, not a private correction. A mistake to avoid: updating a document in the Dashboard without separately informing the Agent and Supplier — the source requires both, not just the record update.',
    4, 'Purchase Order Update Procedure — Purchasing Dashboard SOP, Document Change Management section'),
  lesson('pur-m5-l2', 'pur-m5', 2, 'The Exact Status Vocabulary Used in the Sources',
    'The sources use a small, specific set of named statuses, and this programme preserves them exactly as written rather than substituting more familiar-sounding alternatives. An order that has not been acknowledged by its supplier within the required window is identified as an "Unacknowledged Order." An order that has passed its Expected Completion Date without being finished is described as "overdue." A container item pulled out for rework is reassigned to "Unassigned" status. Once a container is confirmed loaded, its status is updated to "SHIPPING," and once every final check is complete, it is marked "SHIPPED." These five terms are the only status vocabulary this programme teaches, because they are the only ones stated directly in the sources — no other status name (such as "closed" or "cancelled") is invented or assumed. A mistake to avoid: assuming a status name from a different context applies here — use only the five terms above.',
    5, 'Order Follow-Up, Production Monitoring & Container Loading Policy SOP, Order Acknowledgement Verification, Rework Management, and Shipping Status Update sections'),
  lesson('pur-m5-l3', 'pur-m5', 3, 'Update Evidence and Revision History',
    'Every update to a Purchase Order or its compliance documents leaves a trail in the sources\' required process: the new version is recorded in the Dashboard, the Agent and Supplier are informed, and their receipt of the update is confirmed before work continues. The source is direct about what happens if this discipline slips: "old versions must not be used after approval of new versions" — meaning a Supplier or Agent working from an out-of-date document after a newer one has been approved is treated as a process failure, not a minor slip. This is the same evidence discipline introduced in Module 1, now applied specifically to PO and document changes. A mistake to avoid: assuming an update is finished once the Dashboard is edited — the source treats "informed and confirmed" as part of the update itself, not a separate follow-up task.',
    4, 'Purchase Order Update Procedure — Purchasing Dashboard SOP, Document Change Management section'),
  lesson('pur-m5-l4', 'pur-m5', 4, 'Handover and Audit Trail',
    'The source requires that anything a Supplier or Agent must remember, follow, verify, or monitor be recorded in the Purchase Order\'s Remarks Section rather than communicated only in conversation — the exact reasoning given is that this preserves "traceability and accountability." The same logic applies to supplier-related issues, which must be recorded in an Incident Log capturing the supplier name, PO number, issue description, date identified, corrective action requested, supplier response, resolution status, and the person responsible for following it up. Together, the Remarks Section and the Incident Log form the audit trail that lets a later reviewer understand what happened and why, without needing to ask the person who handled it. A mistake to avoid: relying on memory or a private note instead of the Remarks Section or Incident Log — neither is considered "recorded" until it is in the shared Dashboard record.',
    4, 'Purchase Order Update Procedure — Purchasing Dashboard SOP, Purchase Order Remarks Requirements and Incident Log Requirements sections'),

  // Module 6 — Supplier Follow-Up and Production Control
  lesson('pur-m6-l1', 'pur-m6', 1, 'Order Acknowledgement and the 7-Day Rule',
    'Once a Purchase Order has been created and sent to a Supplier, the source requires verifying — within 7 days of PO creation — that the Supplier has acknowledged, reviewed, and accepted the order. If that acknowledgement has not happened within the 7-day window, the order is identified as an "Unacknowledged Order," and the required response is immediate: inform the assigned Agent, request Supplier confirmation, follow up until acknowledgement is received, and update the Purchasing Dashboard throughout. This is a firm, numeric rule stated directly in the source, not a general reminder to "check in eventually." A mistake to avoid: waiting past the 7-day window before taking any action — the source requires the follow-up to begin as soon as the window has passed, not once the delay has become obviously serious.',
    5, 'Order Follow-Up, Production Monitoring & Container Loading Policy SOP, Order Acknowledgement Verification section'),
  lesson('pur-m6-l2', 'pur-m6', 2, 'Expected Completion Dates and Overdue Orders',
    'For every acknowledged order, the source requires confirming that the Supplier has entered an Expected Completion Date and that it has been recorded in the Dashboard — an order with no Expected Completion Date "must be followed up immediately." Once that date has passed without the order being finished, the order is treated as "overdue," and the response is to contact the Agent, request a production status update, request a revised completion date if needed, and record everything in the Dashboard. The source does not give a specific number of days that defines a "long delay" requiring escalation beyond routine follow-up — that judgement is left open in the source itself, and this programme does not invent a number for it. A mistake to avoid: treating "overdue" as a status that only matters once a supplier is seriously late — the source applies it from the day the Expected Completion Date is missed.',
    5, 'Order Follow-Up, Production Monitoring & Container Loading Policy SOP, Expected Completion Date Verification and Overdue Order Monitoring sections'),
  lesson('pur-m6-l3', 'pur-m6', 3, 'Finished Production and Readiness for Loading',
    'Once production is reported complete, the source requires verifying the final quantities, that the product itself is ready, that packaging is complete, and that inspection has taken place. The source is direct about the reason speed matters here: "finished products should be loaded into containers as soon as possible — suppliers should not be kept waiting unnecessarily after production completion," because "maintaining supplier trust and supplier relationships is critical for future order priority and production support." In other words, this is not only an internal efficiency concern — how promptly finished goods are collected affects the relationship with the supplier going forward. A mistake to avoid: treating "production is finished" as the end of this team\'s involvement — the source treats prompt collection and loading as part of the same responsibility.',
    4, 'Order Follow-Up, Production Monitoring & Container Loading Policy SOP, Finished Production Monitoring section'),
  lesson('pur-m6-l4', 'pur-m6', 4, 'Inspection, Rework, and Escalation Evidence',
    'When an inspection finds an issue — a product, packaging, carton, quality, or label problem — the finding must be documented in the Inspection Records, the Packing List, and the Purchasing Dashboard. If the item needs rework, the source requires a specific sequence: remove it from its current container allocation, change its container assignment to "Unassigned," record the rework details, notify the Supplier, notify the Agent, and reload it into a container only after the rework has been approved. The source states this prohibition directly: "products requiring rework must not be shipped." A mistake to avoid: leaving a product needing rework in its original container assignment "for now" — the source requires it to be actively reassigned to "Unassigned" while the rework is resolved, not left in place.',
    4, 'Order Follow-Up, Production Monitoring & Container Loading Policy SOP, Inspection Requirements and Rework Management sections'),

  // Module 7 — Purchase Order Decision Intelligence
  lesson('pur-m7-l1', 'pur-m7', 1, 'What a Decision-Support Tool Is For',
    'Alongside the written Purchasing policies, the source material also includes a set of decision-support tools — written rules, a specification document, and calculation workbooks — built to help judge whether a specific product is worth reordering, based on factors like its profitability, how much container space it uses relative to its value, how quickly it turns into cash, and how risky it is to keep in stock. The stated purpose of these tools is to give a starting recommendation, using a consistent set of factors, faster than working through each factor by hand for every product. That purpose is the only thing this module treats as settled: several of the specific numbers and rules inside these tools disagree with each other across the different source documents, and this programme does not teach any of the disputed detail — see the next three lessons and the Programme Sources page for exactly what is and is not included. A mistake to avoid: assuming every number that appears inside one of these tools has been confirmed correct — this module explains why that assumption does not hold here.',
    5, 'PO Decision Engine Rules v2.4, introductory/purpose section; PO Decision Engine Specification Update v2.4'),
  lesson('pur-m7-l2', 'pur-m7', 2, 'The Kinds of Information These Tools Use',
    'Without stating any disputed formula, the source material is consistent about the general categories of information a decision-support tool like this needs: a product\'s selling price and cost, how much container space (CBM) it takes up per unit, current and incoming stock levels, and its recent sales pattern. Based on factors like these, the source-backed concept is that a product can be scored and grouped into one of four bands, from strongest to weakest — the specific cutoff points for these four bands are one of the few numeric details that stay consistent across every source reviewed, which is why this programme includes the band structure itself while excluding the disputed calculation that produces the underlying score. A mistake to avoid: assuming that because the four-band structure is consistent, the detailed score behind it is also settled — the calculation itself is one of the excluded items in this programme (see Programme Sources).',
    5, 'PO Decision Engine Rules v2.4, Tier Classification section'),
  lesson('pur-m7-l3', 'pur-m7', 3, 'Recommendation Versus Human Decision',
    'None of the decision-support source documents describe a human sign-off, override, or approval step built into the tool itself — every output described in those documents is an automatic label such as approved, blocked, needs review, or recommended for removal, with no named person checking it before it takes effect. This programme treats that gap as important rather than ignoring it: any output from one of these tools is treated as a starting suggestion only, and it must still go through the same human approval steps already taught elsewhere in this programme — for example, Warehouse Team review before a Purchase Order enters the system, and management approval for the specific exception conditions described in Module 4. A mistake to avoid: treating a decision-support tool\'s output as if it were already an approved purchasing decision — under the sources reviewed, it is not, because nothing describes it being checked by a person first.',
    5, 'PO Decision Engine Rules v2.4, action-code sections (no manual override, approval, rejection, or escalation workflow is documented anywhere in this source)'),
  lesson('pur-m7-l4', 'pur-m7', 4, 'Known Limitations of the Prototype Tools',
    'Two different versions of the same decision-support rules exist in the source material, and they define some of the same numbered checks differently from each other — for example, they disagree on what a specific numbered check is even supposed to measure, and on whether some checks are switched on or still awaiting activation. This programme does not use any of those disputed check numbers or definitions anywhere. One of the supporting calculation workbooks was also found, on review, to contain internal calculation defects — including a formula that does not subtract the value it is labelled as subtracting, and a results section where the labels and the numbers underneath them do not line up correctly. None of this disputed or defective material is taught anywhere in this programme (see Programme Sources for the full list), and it is exactly why the previous lesson insists that every output from these tools needs a human check before being acted on. A mistake to avoid: assuming a tool labelled with a version number is automatically more reliable than an older one — in this source set, the newer version introduced new disagreements rather than resolving the old ones.',
    5, 'PO Decision Engine Specification Update v2.4; Container Scoring Model workbook — see Programme Sources for the full exclusion register'),

  // Module 8 — Container Planning and Scoring
  lesson('pur-m8-l1', 'pur-m8', 1, 'Why Container Planning Matters',
    'A container has a fixed amount of usable space, and the sources describe container planning as the process of deciding which products go into which container, and in what order, so that space is used well and the right products arrive when they are needed. The sources describe a numeric capacity target for planning purposes, but the exact figure is not consistent across the documents reviewed — different sources state different numbers for the same idea — so this programme deliberately does not teach a specific capacity figure; the Programme Sources page explains why. What is consistent, and what this programme does teach, is the underlying purpose: plan loading around genuine priority and readiness, not simply "whatever is ready first." A mistake to avoid: assuming a specific container-capacity number seen in one place is the confirmed target — treat container-capacity figures as unconfirmed in this programme.',
    4, 'Order Follow-Up, Production Monitoring & Container Loading Policy SOP, Container Planning Policy section'),
  lesson('pur-m8-l2', 'pur-m8', 2, 'Inputs Used to Plan a Container',
    'Container planning draws on a specific, unambiguous set of inputs: which products are finished and ready, their carton quantities, their weight, and how much volume (CBM) each one takes up. These inputs feed into two separate decisions — which products should be prioritised for the next available container, and how they should physically be arranged once loaded. Both of these decisions are covered in the next lesson. A mistake to avoid: planning a container using only one input (for example, "whichever supplier finished first") when the sources describe several inputs working together — readiness, quantity, weight, and volume all play a role.',
    4, 'Order Follow-Up, Production Monitoring & Container Loading Policy SOP, Finished Production Monitoring and Packing List Verification sections'),
  lesson('pur-m8-l3', 'pur-m8', 3, 'Loading Priority and Sequence Rules',
    'The source gives a specific, ordered list of what should be prioritised for the next available container: urgent stock requirements first, then products urgently required for replenishment, then products whose production is already finished, then any outstanding balance left over from a previous container, and finally other strategic supplier priorities. Separately, the source gives a physical loading rule: heavier cargo should be positioned deeper inside the container, while lighter cargo is loaded first, near the front — meaning the loading sequence and the priority order are two different, related decisions, not the same thing. A mistake to avoid: confusing "which supplier gets priority for the next container" (the priority list) with "where inside the container a product physically goes" (the weight-based loading rule) — both matter, but they answer different questions.',
    4, 'Order Follow-Up, Production Monitoring & Container Loading Policy SOP, Container Planning Policy and Container Loading Order Rules sections'),
  lesson('pur-m8-l4', 'pur-m8', 4, 'Reviewing Recommendations and Handling Exceptions',
    'As Module 7 explained, one of the source workbooks used for container-related scoring was found, on review, to contain internal calculation defects. Because of this, any spreadsheet-based container recommendation must be checked by a person before it is acted on — a plan is never finalised purely because a spreadsheet shows a pass result. The sources also describe a specific exception path: if space limitations prevent a planned item from being loaded, it is removed from the current container, assigned to the next available one, and the records are updated to reflect the change — this is a normal, expected outcome, not a failure, as long as it is documented. A mistake to avoid: treating a container scoring tool\'s "pass" result as confirmation that no further check is needed — see Module 7 for why that assumption does not hold here.',
    4, 'Order Follow-Up, Production Monitoring & Container Loading Policy SOP, Final Loading Verification section; Container Scoring Model workbook — see Programme Sources'),

  // Module 9 — Container Arrival and Stock Receipt
  lesson('pur-m9-l1', 'pur-m9', 1, 'Preparing for Container Arrival',
    'Before a container arrives, the source requires that the team handling receiving be given everything they need in advance: the Final Packing List, the Container Loading File, Shipping Marks, the Product List, carton quantities, and any special unloading instructions — the source is explicit that these documents "must" be received before the container arrives, not on the day it turns up. Where a receiving location needs to be chosen from more than one option, that choice must also be reviewed and confirmed before arrival, not decided on the spot. A mistake to avoid: sending arrival documents to the receiving team on the same day the container is due — the source requires this information in advance so the team has time to prepare.',
    4, 'Container Arrival, Stock Receipt & Inventory Update Policy SOP, Document Distribution to Warehouse Team and Warehouse Allocation sections'),
  lesson('pur-m9-l2', 'pur-m9', 2, 'Receiving and Verifying Quantities',
    'On receipt, the source requires verifying the container number, the seal number, and the number of cartons against the Packing List, along with the quantities actually received. Any shortage, damage, or missing carton must be reported immediately — the source does not describe a delay or a batching of these reports; the instruction is to report them as they are found. This immediate-reporting rule is what allows a discrepancy to be investigated while the container and its paperwork are both still fresh, rather than days later when the details are harder to reconstruct. A mistake to avoid: waiting until the full container has been unloaded and checked before reporting the first discrepancy found — the source requires reporting shortages and damages immediately, not at the end of the process.',
    4, 'Container Arrival, Stock Receipt & Inventory Update Policy SOP, Container Receipt Procedure section'),
  lesson('pur-m9-l3', 'pur-m9', 3, 'Handling Missing, Damaged, Excess or Mismatched Stock',
    'The source names specific categories of receiving discrepancy — missing cartons, damaged cartons, damaged products, quantity shortages, incorrect products, and incorrect labels — and requires the same response for each: record the issue, inform the Purchasing Team, inform the Warehouse Team, inform the Supplier if required, update the Incident Log, and follow up until the issue is resolved. Naming the discrepancy type specifically (rather than logging it as a generic "problem") is what allows the Incident Log to be useful later, both for resolving this particular issue and for reviewing a supplier\'s pattern of issues over time, as described in Module 4. A mistake to avoid: logging a discrepancy without specifying which of these categories it falls into — a vague description makes both the immediate resolution and the later supplier review harder.',
    4, 'Container Arrival, Stock Receipt & Inventory Update Policy SOP, Receiving Discrepancy Management section'),
  lesson('pur-m9-l4', 'pur-m9', 4, 'Closing the Loop: Stock Updates and Handover',
    'Once quantities are confirmed, the source requires updating the inventory system and notifying the relevant internal team that stock has been updated and specific key products are now available — this confirms to the rest of the business that products are ready for sales and operations, not just that they have physically arrived. Where a product had been temporarily substituted while out of stock, that temporary substitution must be removed once the real product is available again — the source is explicit that "temporary mappings must not remain active after stock replenishment." The whole receiving process is only considered complete once every item on the source\'s own Final Receiving Checklist has been confirmed. A mistake to avoid: leaving a temporary out-of-stock substitution active after the real product has arrived and been updated in the system — it must be actively removed, not left to expire on its own.',
    4, 'Container Arrival, Stock Receipt & Inventory Update Policy SOP, Stock-In Update Procedure, Out-of-Stock Mapping Removal Procedure, and Final Receiving Checklist sections'),

  // Module 10 — Exceptions, Evidence and Final Operational Review
  lesson('pur-m10-l1', 'pur-m10', 1, 'Recognising When Something Needs Escalation',
    'Across every module in this programme, the same underlying pattern reappears in different forms: a specific, named condition (an unacknowledged order, an overdue order, a supplier issue, a rework requirement, a receiving discrepancy) triggers a specific, required response, usually involving informing a named role and recording the issue. Recognising the pattern matters more than memorising every individual trigger, because the response is consistent: identify what happened, record it where the team can see it, inform the people who need to know, and follow up until it is resolved. A mistake to avoid: treating every problem as unique and improvising a response — the sources consistently point to the same record-and-inform pattern regardless of which stage of the process the problem occurs in.',
    4, 'Programme-wide synthesis — see Order Follow-Up, Production Monitoring & Container Loading Policy SOP and Container Arrival, Stock Receipt & Inventory Update Policy SOP'),
  lesson('pur-m10-l2', 'pur-m10', 2, 'Shipment and Receipt Exceptions',
    'Two specific exception paths recur across this programme and are worth reviewing together: rework (an inspection finds a problem before shipping, so the item is pulled from its container, reassigned to "Unassigned," and only reloaded once rework is approved) and receiving discrepancies (a shortage, damage, or mismatch is found on arrival, reported immediately, and logged with a specific category). Both exceptions share the same shape — stop the normal flow, document precisely what is wrong, involve the right people, and only resume once the issue is actually resolved, not once it has simply been noted. A mistake to avoid: resuming the normal process (reloading a reworked item, or accepting a discrepant delivery as-is) before the required approval or resolution step has actually happened.',
    4, 'Order Follow-Up, Production Monitoring & Container Loading Policy SOP, Rework Management section; Container Arrival, Stock Receipt & Inventory Update Policy SOP, Receiving Discrepancy Management section'),
  lesson('pur-m10-l3', 'pur-m10', 3, 'Working Safely with Decision-Support Warnings',
    'Module 7 established that none of the decision-support tools in the source material describe a human check built into the tool itself, and that one of the supporting workbooks contains confirmed calculation defects. The practical consequence, repeated here as a final reminder, is this: whenever this programme\'s content or a real workplace tool presents a recommendation, score, or "pass" result generated by one of these tools, it is treated as a starting suggestion, not a finished decision — the same Warehouse Team and management approval steps taught throughout this programme still apply on top of it. A mistake to avoid: letting a confident-looking score or a green "pass" label substitute for the human review steps taught elsewhere in this programme.',
    4, 'PO Decision Engine Rules v2.4 and Container Scoring Model workbook — see Module 7 and Programme Sources'),
  lesson('pur-m10-l4', 'pur-m10', 4, 'Final Review and Preparing for the Practical Task',
    'This programme has covered the Purchasing Team\'s responsibilities, the shared end-to-end workflow, the inputs behind a purchasing decision, PO creation and updates, supplier follow-up, decision-support tools and their limits, container planning, and stock receipt — always preserving the sources\' exact terminology, sequence, and named conditions, and always excluding the specific figures and rules the sources disagree about. The final practical task asks you to apply this knowledge to a short, fictional purchasing scenario. It is entirely PROTOTYPE_ONLY: it does not create or send a real Purchase Order, does not contact a real supplier, and is not an approval, certification, or sign-off of any kind. Completing it is optional and does not affect your programme completion, which is based only on finishing every lesson and passing all 10 Skill Checks.',
    3, 'Programme-wide synthesis; see the Final Practical Task screen for details'),
];
